A||||10||Eric||Does anyone not have this problem?
Z||000000||Eric||05-23-99||12:09 PM||||As many of you already know, I have been suffering with chronic clutch problems.  I thought my troubles were hydralic only, but it seems as if the whole system is working against me.  I replaced the master cylinder, slave cylinder, and the complete clutch line, only to find that the car still doesn't work any better.  I had plans to rebuild the motor, upgrade the interior and give the exterior some attention too, but now I would just be satisfied with a car that just goes into gear.  I can't stand to even look at the car anymore.  It sits there in a puddle of brake fluid, laughing at me.  <p>I memorized the thread "clutch info uncovered..." and still can't figure out what to throw a few hundred bucks at next.  It would be reassuring to hear from a few people with manual transmissions who do not have a problem with shifting and the clutch in general.  This thing has got me at the breaking point and if I don't feel better about it soon, the next time you will see my car will be in the local auto trader. 
Z||000001||Patrick||05-23-99||12:49 PM||||Hi Eric,<p>Sorry to hear you're having such a problem. <p>I'd be interested in hearing how you bled the system. If it's not done "properly", no amount of money and replacement of parts will help. There seems to be some disagreement over how to bleed the system "properly". I'm sure you saw my comments on this in "<b>Clutch Info uncovered...</b>"<p><p>      
Z||000002||theogre||05-23-99||12:58 PM||||I can tell you the 84 I'm in the middle of buying has a clutch that works just fine....<p>It still sounds like there is air trapped, or something in the trany is binding or bent. Assuming the clutch pedal itself doesn’t have bent parts.<p>by now you've replaced the entire fluid content of the clutch even if you weren't trying to. That’s a good thing. It removes any metal, water, or other dirt from the system.<p>try this..... For Fiero it's experimental but it might help. In a large horizontal cylinder it's hard to get the air to flow toward the bleed. try parking/jacking the car so that the left side is as high as you can get it. that should point the slave's open end down some. then tap on the slave with a small <b>PLASTIC</b> hammer to shake the bubbles loose. Vent the slave bleeder. tap it a few more times and vent it again. Tap the slave on the closed end, not the side, that way if you tap a bit to hard you won’t knock the slave out of round.<p>I used to do this by jacking the front of a ford truck and it helped. I can’t see any reason for it not to help with any other car. Be careful to remove a bit of fluid from the master cyl before you tilt the car to avoid spilling it during bleeding. <p>Even if you try pushing in the piston like some people do, tapping it should get any air clinging to the other surfaces to float up. <p>Since you did replace the line you might need to fool with it some more…. The air trapped in the front might not all want to flow down to the master. If this is the case it’s a pain…. (I’ve had that problem with replacing break lines…. Very annoying.) The only way to get rid of these some time is to back bleed the line. Once you get all the air out that you can, crack open the nut on the master, push in the slave a bit, and close the nut. It's a bitch to do and if there is any air at all in the master, it will be even harder.<br>
Z||000003||Eric||05-23-99||02:03 PM||||I don't think air is my problem now.  I just bled the system for the second time since I put in the new line and I think I removed all the air because shifting is a lot easier.  <p>The clutch pedal itself feels like it has about two inches of freeplay then starts to work, but I know this isn't the case because when I look down at the clutch arm I can see it start to move imediatly once someone first touches the pedal.  <p>The shifter now feels a ton better.  With the exception of first gear, the entire shift pattern feels precise and fluid ( like it should be ).  However, when I put it into first gear without double clutching, I feel like it pops into gear.  I can actually hear a poping sound.  I think I can live with this problem for now.  Does anybody know what could be causing this?  Could it be a worn clutch ( it has almost 140000 miles on the original ).
Z||000004||Phil||05-23-99||06:03 PM||||Hate to keep bringing up the same point but I had the same problem. Mine turned out to be the alunimum pedal. My clutch arm would start to move when you first started to push the pedal also, but a lot of throw was used up in flexing of the pedal and slop in the banjo fitting. I replaced the pedal with a steel one and put a bushing in the banjo. Now I only have to push the pedal about half way down to shift. 
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||05-23-99||07:41 PM||||I have the same problem. 1st and 2nd are impossible to shift sometimes. I have an 86 SE V6 with the 4 speed and I have 56,000 hard driven miles on the original clutch. I stopped messing with it after I got it to where I could atleast shift. I plan to buy an 88GT now. That should solve all my problems. Plus I have always wanted an 88GT. If it helps any you could try what I did,it doesn't cost a dime. It's a little further down "Does your 4 speed seem like it needs a few more inches of travel?"
Z||000006||Eric||05-23-99||08:49 PM||||What the Hell are you talking about!  An 88 GT won't solve your problem, that's what I have. <p>But I do love it.  Its got everything you could possibly want, including a bum clutch.
Z||000007||theogre||05-24-99||07:43 AM||||only 1 or 2 gears acting up could be the shifter out of whack, pilot bind, clutch drag, or the syncro for one or more gears are acting up. usually if the input shaft is spinning because of pilot bind, or clutch drag, it affects all gears but some times low gears show it up more than the high ones.<p>in the case of first gear, some of them are so deeply geared that if the car is moving at all it might not want to engage. Allot of gear boxes have trouble syncing first gear at any more than about 5mph.<p>If the gear binds when the car isn't moving(engine running) then it's likely an internal problem in the trany, pilot bushing bind, clutch disk drag, or the shifter is a little out.<p>First thing to do is verify the shifter is ok. the procedure for that is in the Haynes book.
Z||000008||Punkndrublic||05-24-99||04:07 PM||||My clutch works near perfectly, the only serious problem i had was someone put in a cheap clutch, the rivets that hold the material on started to losen the the whole piece would shift back and forth, but i hade that replaced when i had my engine rebuilt. I just  hope that i never have any problems close to what your going through.
Z||000009||Eric||05-24-99||08:05 PM||||My problem could be solved with that new line.  I just need to make sure I get all the air out of the system.  Has anyone ever tried presure bleeding?  It may be a better solution than Ogre's back bleeding procedure. 
Z||000010||Patrick||05-24-99||08:34 PM||||<br>Hmmmm....<p>So Eric, sounds like you're not so quick to brush off my concerns about bleeding the system "properly" now. <IMG SRC="http://forums.gagames.com/forums/smile.gif"> <p>I explained in the the thread I referred you to that I "pressure bleed" my system. I use something that I've jury-rigged at home, but I've often wondered if it would be even more effective to have a brake shop pressure bleed the system with their pro equipment.<p>Give it a shot. Good luck.<p><p>
